WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.476
Lesson seven.

00:00:01.476 --> 00:00:03.889
Evolution built a network that lands.

00:00:03.889 --> 00:00:05.980
But what did it actually build?

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:07.781
Let's take it apart.

00:00:08.381 --> 00:00:14.776
One simple way to find out what matters is to switch a connection off, fly again, and see what breaks.

00:00:14.776 --> 00:00:18.738
Here is the winner flying, with all of its connections.

00:00:19.338 --> 00:00:25.819
The most important connection runs from the falling speed to the main engine, with a big negative weight.

00:00:25.819 --> 00:00:32.701
When the lander falls fast, its vertical speed is negative, so the main engine's score shoots up, and it brakes.

00:00:32.701 --> 00:00:37.245
Switch this one connection off, and the lander never lands at all.

00:00:37.845 --> 00:00:40.879
Next, the tilt angle feeds the left engine.

00:00:40.879 --> 00:00:44.507
This is the connection that keeps the lander upright.

00:00:44.507 --> 00:00:48.019
Without it, only five percent of flights land.

00:00:48.619 --> 00:00:50.383
And here is a surprise.

00:00:50.383 --> 00:00:56.814
Once the right leg touches the ground, its sensor tells the engines to stop, and votes for doing nothing.

00:00:56.814 --> 00:01:02.334
Without that, the lander keeps nudging itself and lands only fifteen percent of the time.

00:01:02.334 --> 00:01:05.577
Evolution ignored the left leg sensor completely.

00:01:05.577 --> 00:01:07.414
One leg was enough.

00:01:08.014 --> 00:01:14.212
Here are the connections that matter most, ranked by how much the landing rate drops without them.

00:01:14.212 --> 00:01:16.843
Red bars mean the lander falls apart.

00:01:16.843 --> 00:01:19.599
Green bars mean it barely notices.

00:01:20.199 --> 00:01:24.148
Nine of the seventeen connections can go with almost no loss.

00:01:24.148 --> 00:01:26.896
A handful of them carry the whole skill.

00:01:26.896 --> 00:01:29.414
That's typical for evolved networks.

00:01:29.414 --> 00:01:34.866
They are not tidy, but the important parts are usually few, and you can find them.

00:01:35.466 --> 00:01:39.103
Switching connections off shows what the network relies on.

00:01:39.103 --> 00:01:42.198
Watching where it fails shows what it is missing.

00:01:42.198 --> 00:01:46.432
On a thousand new flights, it landed ninety-seven percent of the time.

00:01:46.432 --> 00:01:52.432
Sixteen of its eighteen crashes ended to the right of the pad, where it tipped over on the slope.

00:01:53.032 --> 00:01:55.271
The other failures are stranger.

00:01:55.271 --> 00:01:58.686
Fourteen flights ran out of time, sitting on the pad.

00:01:58.686 --> 00:02:01.876
In eight of them, only the left leg was touching.

00:02:01.876 --> 00:02:05.123
Remember, the network ignores its left leg sensor.

00:02:05.123 --> 00:02:10.181
It waits for the right leg, and keeps nudging itself until the time runs out.

00:02:10.781 --> 00:02:14.285
And every failure began with a hard sideways push.

00:02:14.285 --> 00:02:18.756
A network with no hidden nodes handles the usual flight very well.

00:02:18.756 --> 00:02:23.829
These rare, hard starts are where a better network could still improve.
